g class=\"sig\">170 record highs</strong>, far more than the 90 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (82). Punta Gorda's all-time high is 101\u00b0F, on June 17, 1981.","t_chg":"Punta Gorda's nights have warmed since 1985. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 92 complete years: Punta Gorda's average low shows no clear change: +0.01\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.08 to +0.10) that includes zero. Punta Gorda's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.17\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.25), about +1.8\u00b0F since 1915. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1985, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.34\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.67) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.42\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.69). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months; January fell. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Punta Gorda's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.06 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 51.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1947 (88.10 in); the driest, 2007 (30.01 in). Punta Gorda almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, 5 daily record lows"}}